Open an archive's file with its default app (Windows' "Open with" dialog when there is
none). The entry is extracted, with the archive's Mark of the Web, into a folder of its own
under `%TEMP%/BeeZip/open`, so two entries of one name don't collide; folders there older
than a day are deleted as it goes. Programs run as they would from Explorer: SmartScreen sees
the mark.

Usage: beezip open [OPTIONS] <ARCHIVE> <ENTRY>

Arguments:
  <ARCHIVE>
          The archive

  <ENTRY>
          The file inside it, as listed

Options:
  -p, --password <PASSWORD>
          The password, if the entry is encrypted

      --name-encoding <ENCODING>
          How names stored as bytes are read, so `entry` matches them as listed [default: from
          config, `auto`]
          
          [possible values: auto, utf-8, cp437, cp850, cp852, cp866, cp874, cp932, cp936, cp949,
          cp950, cp1250, cp1251, cp1252, cp1253, cp1254, cp1255, cp1256, cp1257, cp1258, koi8-r,
          koi8-u, iso-8859-1, iso-8859-2, iso-8859-5, iso-8859-7, euc-jp, gb18030]

      --launch[=<LAUNCH>]
          Open the extracted file; `false` only extracts it, for the caller to open [default: true]
          
          [possible values: true, false]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --output <FORMAT>
          How to print results; ndjson streams every job event

          Possible values:
          - human:  Text for people
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout: the output, or `{"error": {...}}`
          - ndjson: Every job event as a JSON line on stdout, ending with `Finished`, `Failed` or
            `Cancelled`
          
          [default: human]

  -q, --quiet
          No progress bars or info messages; warnings still print
